From 287518868a2289657c2a5a6a3d68f3658822d9d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 09:39:01 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: label non-resident pages, pace async publication,
 reap vectored AIO

A no-fault copy fails on a page that is mapped but has no page-table entry
yet, for example a string in a freshly forked child's .rodata, although
the kernel faults that page in for the syscall. Such fields were recorded
as ANT_COPY_FAULT, the same as an unmapped pointer. When the whole failed
range lies in readable mappings of the caller (checked under
mmap_read_trylock), the field is now recorded as ANT_NOT_RESIDENT. The
recorder still never faults; ranges it cannot classify stay COPY_FAULT.

Async records are published by one worker in a burst. On the CPU it ran
on, the burst could fill the telemetry ring and displace the syscall
events of tasks running there. The worker now waits, up to 10 ms per
record, until that CPU's ring is at most half full. ant_send_room()
reports the free space of the calling CPU's ring for this purpose.

A vectored AIO read that completes in interrupt context left its output
unobserved: the reap table held one buffer. The table now keeps a copy of
the iovec array the kernel imported and reads those buffers at reap, so a
later change to the process's own array cannot redirect the capture.
